    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a bit too much for my stakeholder. Can you rewrite it without the metaphors? Just give me the actual technical definitions for vertical and horizontal scaling in a professional tone.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. Vertical scaling, also known as \"scaling up,\" involves increasing the capacity of a single server or instance by adding more resources, such as CPU, memory, or storage. This approach can improve performance and handle increased workloads, but it has limitations due to the maximum capacity of a single server.\n\nHorizontal scaling, or \"scaling out,\" involves adding more servers or instances to a pool to distribute the workload across multiple resources. This method allows for greater flexibility and scalability, as additional servers can be added or removed based on demand. Horizontal scaling is often preferred for handling large-scale applications and traffic spikes.\n\nAuto Scaling groups in AWS are designed to automatically adjust the number of instances in response to changes in demand. When traffic spikes, the Auto Scaling group can launch additional instances to handle the increased workload. Conversely, when traffic decreases, the Auto Scaling group can terminate unnecessary instances to optimize resource usage and cost. This ensures that the application can handle varying levels of traffic while maintaining performance and efficiency.",
